<spell-notebook "meta usage">
	<header level="1">
		Meta
		<header>A meta holds metadata about the content, such as a date or a category.</>
	</>
	<meta-tab-bar showing="usage"/>
	<section>
		<menu class="ui-docs-section-tabs" appearance="segmented" aria-label="Sections">
			<item link="#using-it">Using it</>
			<item link="#owners">Owners</>
			<item link="#accessibility">Accessibility</>
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Using it">
		<markdown>
			`<ui-meta>` holds metadata about its owner's content, such as a date or a category: a card's or an item's meta line,
			a feed event's likes, a comment's `metadata`.

			- No attributes: the owner decides the look (small and muted almost everywhere).
			- Put each piece in its own `<span>` or `<a>`: an item spaces them apart, a comment separates them.
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Owners">
		<markdown>
			`<ui-meta>` finds its nearest owner and sets `:state(in-<owner>)` on itself; `UIParts.css` styles it by that
			state. Its owners, and what each makes of it:

			- [Card](/components/ui/ui-card.html) (`:state(in-card)`): small, muted, under the header
			- [Item](/components/ui/ui-items.html) (`:state(in-item)`): small, muted, each piece spaced apart
			- [Feed](/components/ui/ui-feed.html) (`:state(in-feed)`): a small line under the summary (likes, replies)
			- [Comment](/components/ui/ui-comment.html) (`:state(in-comment)`): inline after the author, small and muted

			Anywhere else it is an unstyled block. More on how parts find their owners: [Content parts](/components/ui/ui-header.html).
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Accessibility">
		<markdown>
			- A neutral block with no role. Its muted colour is a look only: dates belong in `<time>` (or a `<ui-date
			  datetime>` in a feed) for machines.
		</>
	</>
</spell-notebook>
